          "type": "concept",
          "title": "The State of Nature: Hobbes vs. Locke",
          "content": "Political philosophy begins with a thought experiment: imagine humans before any government. What does that **'state of nature'** look like?\n\n**Thomas Hobbes (1588–1679)** — author of *Leviathan* (1651) — answered grimly. With no common power, life would be a 'war of all against all' and human existence would be **'solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short.'** People escape this terror by surrendering nearly all their rights to a powerful sovereign in exchange for security.\n\n**John Locke (1632–1704)** — *Two Treatises of Government* (1689) — disagreed. The state of nature is governed by **natural law** discoverable through reason. People are born with **natural rights** to:\n• **Life**\n• **Liberty** (freedom from arbitrary control)\n• **Property** (which Locke meant broadly — what your labor produces)\n\nGovernment is created by the people to protect these rights more reliably than they could on their own. If government violates the trust, the people may alter or abolish it.\n\nThe American founders picked Locke — not Hobbes. That choice is etched into every line of the Declaration of Independence.",
