          "type": "concept",
          "title": "The Social Contract",
          "content": "Locke's framework gives us the master idea of liberal democracy: the **social contract**.\n\n**The deal in plain English:**\n1. Humans possess rights before any government exists.\n2. To protect those rights more securely, free people **consent** to form a government and give it limited powers.\n3. Government holds those powers as a **trust**.\n4. If government breaks the trust — by attacking the rights it was created to defend — the people have the right to **alter or abolish it** and build a new one.\n\n**Jean-Jacques Rousseau (1712–1778)** added a key twist in *The Social Contract* (1762): legitimate government must reflect the **general will** of the people, not just the will of rulers.\n\nThis is why the American system insists on:\n• **Consent of the governed** (elections, referenda)\n• **Limited government** (enumerated powers, Bill of Rights)\n• **A right to reform** (amendment, peaceful protest, the ballot box)\n\nThe contract is invisible — no one signs it — but you are inside it the moment you live under American law.",
