          "explanation": "Federalist No. 51 is Madison's defense of checks and balances. The famous lines: 'If men were angels, no government would be necessary' and 'Ambition must be made to counteract ambition.' Federalist No. 10 (also Madison) defends the large republic against faction. Federalist No. 70 (Hamilton) defends a single executive. Federalist No. 78 (Hamilton) defends judicial review. All are essential — but No. 51 is the checks-and-balances essay.",
