          "explanation": "True threats are unprotected speech under cases like Virginia v. Black (2003) — statements where the speaker means to communicate a serious intent to commit unlawful violence. Other unprotected categories include incitement to imminent lawless action (Brandenburg), defamation, obscenity, and child pornography. Criticism of policy is core protected political speech. Academic discussion of past acts is protected. Flag burning is symbolic speech protected by Texas v. Johnson (1989). 'Free speech' is broad — but it has clear edges.",
