          "type": "practice",
          "title": "Practice: Follow a Current Bill Through Congress",
          "content": "**Exercise.** Visit congress.gov. Pick ONE active bill in the current Congress on a topic you care about. Track it through 30 days using the worksheet below. Submit the worksheet at the end of the period.\n\n**Worksheet structure:**\n\n**Day 1 — Setup**\n• **Bill number** (e.g., H.R. 1234 or S. 567):\n• **Bill title:**\n• **Sponsor:** (party, state, district)\n• **Cosponsors:** (count, party breakdown)\n• **Committee(s) of jurisdiction:**\n• **Sign up for alerts** on congress.gov.\n• **One-sentence summary** of what the bill does (use the CRS summary, NOT a partisan source):\n\n**Day 1 — Position assessment**\n• Read the **CRS summary** in full. Note key provisions.\n• Identify the **CBO score** if one exists. Cost over 10 years:\n• Identify your **own position** with reasons. Write one paragraph.\n\n**Days 1-30 — Track**\n• **Action 1** (date + action):\n• **Action 2:**\n• **Action 3:**\n• (continue as needed)\n\n**Day 15 — Mid-track check**\n• Has the bill moved? What stage is it at?\n• Has your representative taken a position?\n• What are committee members saying?\n\n**Day 30 — Final report**\n• **Final stage** of the bill at end of period:\n• **Probability** the bill becomes law in this Congress (your estimate, with reasoning):\n• **Two actions** you took as a citizen during the tracking period (e.g., called a member, attended a hearing, wrote a letter, posted analysis, talked to neighbors):\n• **One thing** you learned about Congress that you did not know before:\n\n**Model output (illustrative).**\n*Bill: H.R. 1234, the 'Example Skills Training Act.' Sponsor: Rep. Smith (D-CA-12). 27 cosponsors (24 D, 3 R). Referred to House Education and Workforce Committee. CRS summary: expands Pell Grant eligibility for short-term workforce training programs. CBO score: $8.4 billion over 10 years.*\n\n*Mid-track: bill received committee hearing on Day 12. Committee chair Rep. Jones (R-NC-3) signaled openness but wants offsets. I called Rep. Jones's office Day 14 to support the bill with a personal story about my niece's training program.*\n\n*Day 30 final: bill remains in committee. Probability of becoming law this Congress: maybe 15-20% in current form, higher if amended for offsets. Two actions: phone call to Jones; letter to my own member supporting cosponsoring. Learning: most legislative debate happens in the committee, not on the floor; one call from a constituent gets logged.*\n\n**Why the exercise matters.** Citizens who can do this for ONE bill have done more legislative tracking than 95% of voters. Repeat for two or three bills per Congress, and you become genuinely informed — the kind of citizen the framers needed and the kind every member of Congress notices.",
