          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "REVIEW (Lessons 9-10 — Elections and Campaign Finance): In Citizens United v. FEC (2010), the Supreme Court held that the First Amendment prohibits the government from restricting independent campaign expenditures by which entities?",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "Foreign governments and foreign nationals",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Corporations and labor unions",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Individual donors only",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Political parties exclusively",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "explanation": "Citizens United v. FEC (2010) held 5-4 that corporations and labor unions, like individuals, have First Amendment rights to make independent political expenditures — that is, spending NOT coordinated with a candidate's campaign. The decision struck down a federal law banning corporate and union independent expenditures. It led to the rise of Super PACs, which can accept unlimited contributions from corporations, unions, and individuals as long as they do not coordinate with candidates. Foreign governments and foreign nationals remain prohibited from political spending. Direct contributions to candidates from corporations and unions remain prohibited; only independent expenditures are permitted. The decision is one of the most consequential and controversial campaign-finance rulings in American history.",
